WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:00.960
Speaker 1: From scrutiny.

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.280
Speaker 1: Except Christianity, of course.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.920
Speaker 1: If a Christian is going to make a claim, we ask them for facts.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:07.360
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.880
Speaker 1: Now, for many of these religions, we don't ask them for facts.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:19.200
Speaker 1: So what is the hardcore evidence to know that this thing that you believe in is true?

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:38.050
Speaker 1: The moment you start pushing them towards something tangible that you can hold and believe in a factual sense, that moment they start realizing that, well, I think what I've been doing all along is that I just know and I believe that is true.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.570
Speaker 1: But that is where that's a good place to actually introduce Christianity.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:47.370
Speaker 1: That Christianity did not just tell you that, oh, just trust in God and just trust in Jesus.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.290
Speaker 1: Actually, there's a real man.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.340
Speaker 1: Jesus Christ lived at a particular time, historically traceable.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.300
Speaker 1: His claims are historically traceable.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:06.100
Speaker 1: When he said he died, the death, the resurrection, these are claims that we can actually trace historically.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.700
Speaker 1: In fact, next week we are going to be going through how to actually defend the resurrection.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.700
Speaker 1: I think you should look forward to that because then you will realize that the resurrection does not need to believed by just faith.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.450
Speaker 2: Facts back it up.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:28.530
Speaker 1: The same way we can investigate a murder, we can investigate if Christ truly died and was truly raised from the dead.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:32.970
Speaker 1: Now that to someone who is objective is something to consider.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.730
Speaker 1: Something to consider, something to consider.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:35.490
Speaker 2: Powerful stuff.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:37.570
Speaker 2: Now, the next way to evangelize.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.650
Speaker 2: And this is why evangelism must never be a checkbox activity.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.170
Speaker 2: It requires emotional investment.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.580
Speaker 2: You need to find what this new age's deepest desire is.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:59.859
Speaker 2: Because if you find what that desire is, you can bring meaning to it through Christ, for example, just like Jesus at the well in John chapter four, him engaging that woman about water.

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:01.500
Speaker 2: I think it's a poetic.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.980
Speaker 2: It's such a poetic and prophetic image that he met her by the well.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:06.780
Speaker 1: Exactly.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:11.340
Speaker 2: To just picture what her real desire was, he asks her for water.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.840
Speaker 2: But what she's looking for is satisfaction.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.200
Speaker 2: And the conversations begin to drive to the emptiness in her life.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.200
Speaker 2: Why will a woman have five husbands?

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:29.120
Speaker 2: And he tells her, if you knew the gift of God and he that is asking you to give him drink, you would have asked him for living water.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.640
Speaker 2: And you will never thirst again.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.200
Speaker 2: That alone triggered the hunger.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.120
Speaker 2: It exposed the need.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.800
Speaker 2: But from conversation, some of them are looking for meaning.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.340
Speaker 2: Once you can find that, you address it.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.700
Speaker 2: Some are looking for community, some have been jaded.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.300
Speaker 2: Some are looking for peace, healing, control, purpose, transcendence, belonging.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.980
Speaker 2: So what does your spirituality give you that you were looking for is the question to ask them.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:07.470
Speaker 2: And from that way you can present the gospel to Them, you know, I happen to have someone close to me who is walking through these beliefs.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.630
Speaker 2: And by the grace of God, I'm going to win.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:11.910
Speaker 2: Christ will win.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:20.310
Speaker 2: And one of the things I discovered was that it was a rebellion to the Christian faith, just a rebellion because of hurt, church and all.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:25.070
Speaker 2: And the person throws the question, what about other faiths?

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:25.750
Speaker 2: What about this?

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.870
Speaker 2: This is not a conversation to have.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:32.630
Speaker 2: And then I begin to bring it in from a practical point, answering the questions of the heart.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.830
Speaker 2: I answer the questions of this, and it opened up their mind.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:41.110
Speaker 2: So this is a very key aspect that we must, we must look at.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:48.390
Speaker 1: You know what makes this so important is this, I think, was years ago, I was having a conversation with someone.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.190
Speaker 1: This was someone who left the faith.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.150
Speaker 1: And I was trying to preach to the person.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:58.950
Speaker 1: I said, christianity is the only faith that truly explains the human condition.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.770
Speaker 1: And not just explains it, answers it.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:10.130
Speaker 1: But the fact that Christianity is the only faith that lets you know that actually the reason why humans behave the way they behave.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:23.650
Speaker 1: There was a theologian I was listening to, and he said when he went to the site of the Rwandan genocide, when he got there, he understood what the Bible says when it says that the heart of man is desperate.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:37.080
Speaker 1: We know this from experience, but somehow no other faith truly explains why the evil we experience in our world exists.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:44.520
Speaker 1: Like, it's quite interesting when you think about all us humans can execute.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:49.160
Speaker 1: When you think about the fact that a father can rape his own daughter.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:05:05.450
Speaker 1: When you think about the fact that people slaughter their fellow humans for nothing but chicken change, just money that they will probably waste in less than a month.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:15.370
Speaker 1: And so the fact that Christianity answers all of this so many times, I find it so profound that there is no angle.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:16.770
Speaker 1: Is it pain?

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:17.530
Speaker 1: Is it hurt?

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.210
Speaker 1: Is this fear of death?

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:26.210
Speaker 1: Christianity answers everything in such a profound way that no other religion comes close.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.610
Speaker 1: Let's come to the matter of the afterlife.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:29.090
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.770
Speaker 1: Every other faith will just let you know that.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:32.450
Speaker 1: Okay, you know what?

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:36.930
Speaker 1: If you do this, if you do that, then probably most likely there is no guarantee.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:37.730
Speaker 2: There's no guarantee.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.810
Speaker 1: It's only Christianity that has a savior.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:44.130
Speaker 1: That said, I went there, I'm back, I have the keys from that.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.690
Speaker 2: You know, I like how the Book of Acts puts it.

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:49.910
Speaker 2: He says the resurrection of Christ has given assurance unto all men.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:57.990
Speaker 2: It means that there is possibility for every man to make the resurrection only if he believes it's a powerful thing.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:07.310
Speaker 2: You know what you were saying about the human condition, there's no other Faith that has presented a transformation like the Christian faith.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.110
Speaker 2: You know, I was listening to a podcaster and he interviewed John Le.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:19.510
Speaker 2: John Lennox on his platform, one of the popular theologians who happens to be a mathematics person and all that.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.470
Speaker 2: So he has proven faith even from science.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:28.430
Speaker 2: And he said to him, said, you are not the first Christian I'm intervening on this platform.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:37.030
Speaker 2: He said, but I've noticed something about every Christian that has come here that I've not seen in anybody in any other faith, no matter how morally disciplined they are.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.150
Speaker 2: And he's asked, what is it?

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:40.060
Speaker 2: He says, a deep sense of calm.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:41.570
Speaker 2: Calmness and satisfaction.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:45.250
Speaker 2: He says, like something has literally changed from the inside out.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:46.530
Speaker 2: He now speaks of his friend.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.050
Speaker 2: He said, my friend had used to be a Buddhist.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.010
Speaker 2: Used to be this, he said, but he became a Christian.

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:55.490
Speaker 2: And he says in 18 months, this person speaking was not a Christian.

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:58.810
Speaker 2: He says, in 18 months he has never been this happy.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.210
Speaker 2: He has never been this calm.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.730
Speaker 2: He says, it's different from anything any therapy has done for me.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:08.040
Speaker 2: So what explains the human condition also brings about the human solution.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.440
Speaker 2: And we must take advantage of this.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:15.760
Speaker 2: That despite the pain anybody is feeling, what are they really looking for in spirituality?

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.240
Speaker 2: Christ provides it and more.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:18.840
Speaker 2: He provides it and more.

00:07:22.439 --> 00:07:30.920
Speaker 1: The next thing you must also do is that you must challenge the idea that all spiritual paths lead to God.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:41.040
Speaker 1: And it's important because until still you leave them with a decision to make, they feel like they have options scattered in whatever they find that they are practicing.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:47.600
Speaker 1: So the moment you can establish that it's actually not logically consistent to say that.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.400
Speaker 1: Because if there is a God, for example, here is pidams.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.920
Speaker 1: Pidams is either black or white.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.080
Speaker 1: It cannot be both.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:07:59.920
Speaker 2: I am black on white.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:04.940
Speaker 1: It's not possible.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:06.180
Speaker 1: It's not possible.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.380
Speaker 1: He either has air or he doesn't.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:10.140
Speaker 1: And so when we start to.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:15.500
Speaker 1: When you introduce the fact that you have to have a logically consistent position, you.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:17.820
Speaker 1: You force them into a decision.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.020
Speaker 1: So it moves from every part, can lead to God to, okay, which one then?

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:23.460
Speaker 2: Which one?

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.060
Speaker 1: And when you get to that point, you are closer.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:25.700
Speaker 2: You're closer.

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:28.620
Speaker 1: So it's very important that you debunk that.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:34.919
Speaker 2: And then just two points present Christ, because the gospel is not preached until Christ is presented.

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.200
Speaker 2: Christ is the revelation of God to the world.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.400
Speaker 2: Present his nature and his mission.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:46.760
Speaker 2: Then show proof of how Jesus proved all he said by his resurrection and how the resurrection truly happened.

00:08:46.919 --> 00:08:47.720
Speaker 2: Prove all of that.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:49.880
Speaker 2: And finally pray with them.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:50.840
Speaker 2: Pray with them.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.560
Speaker 2: According to an ex New Ager, who is now an evangelist.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.970
Speaker 2: He said, prayer is like a spa treatment to the New Ager.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:04.290
Speaker 2: So ensure that you pray for them and that your prayers are for the Spirit of God.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:08.570
Speaker 2: To meet them at the point of their need, be the answer to their underlying desire.

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:17.250
Speaker 2: You see, one of the things that our faith would do to a New Ager is to break the defenses, because many of them already feel like they are God.

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:31.160
Speaker 2: But bringing them to that point that, like Psalm 100, verse 3 says, it is God who made us, not we ourselves, we are his people and the sheep of his pasture, brings them to the vulnerability of the need for God to get them to that point.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:32.960
Speaker 2: That's why it is like a spa treatment.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:43.639
Speaker 2: It just hits the core somewhere that has been blocked for so long and ultimately keep them on a journey of discipleship that will guide them into the truth.

00:09:43.639 --> 00:09:44.119
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:51.880
Speaker 1: And finally, prayer is a tool, in fact, is one of our strongest tools in evangelism.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:53.986
Speaker 1: Look at what the Bible says in Second Corinthians 4.

00:09:54.054 --> 00:10:08.280
Speaker 1: 3 Says, but if our gospel is veiled in is veiled to those who are perishing, whose minds the God of this age has blinded, who do not believe, lest the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is the image of God, should shine on them.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.040
Speaker 1: Meaning for some of these people, it's actually the devil.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:19.320
Speaker 1: So because one thing about the devil is that he will use your legitimate pain to achieve his goal.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:28.410
Speaker 1: So now, because you were ought by church, or you were ought by whatever it was, and so it now places like a veil in your eyes.

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:37.090
Speaker 1: So for some people, it is until you removed that the veil is removed that they will start to see it clearly.

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:42.210
Speaker 1: This is why it is not advisable going to conversations like this with the goat to win an argument.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:44.130
Speaker 1: Yes, you will win the arguments.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:48.050
Speaker 1: Actually, if you listen to this, you win the argument, but will you win the soul?

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:48.490
Speaker 1: No.

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:51.510
Speaker 1: And Jesus sent us to win souls, not argument.

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:56.070
Speaker 1: So please make sure you pray for them and you keep at it.

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:00.550
Speaker 1: And persistence also really matters because they have to know that you actually care.

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:07.270
Speaker 1: It's not like you reached out one day just because, oh, so because I didn't come to church now, not talking to me again, that kind of thing.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:07.550
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:10.630
Speaker 1: So just keep at it and keep on going.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:17.050
Speaker 1: Okay, so now we're going to be talking about African traditional religion.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.210
Speaker 1: Now, the same with New Age practices.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:30.170
Speaker 1: African traditional religion is a combination of several indigenous beliefs and practices that are not necessarily the same.

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:41.930
Speaker 1: But we can trace similar characteristics now, one of these is the fact that they all believe in a supreme being.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:49.580
Speaker 1: So, for instance, if you are Yoruba, then you know that the supreme being of the universe is Yoludumare.

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:50.100
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:53.220
Speaker 1: In Igbo, that is Chukwu.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:57.220
Speaker 1: I mean, Ausa, some call him Circle Al Jin.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:11:58.180
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:04.180
Speaker 1: Then when you come to Ghana, they call the Supreme Being Yame.

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:11.980
Speaker 1: Then when you come to South Africa, the Zulu tribe specifically is called the Umvelinkanji.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.940
Speaker 1: I don't know if I got that correctly.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:17.500
Speaker 1: Sorry if I butchered your language.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:22.220
Speaker 1: When it comes to the Maasai tribe of.

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:24.220
Speaker 1: Of Kenya, it's called the Enkai.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:26.700
Speaker 1: Now, you now notice something.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:28.860
Speaker 1: Every single.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:34.980
Speaker 1: And I think it's quite interesting that every single African traditional religion understands that there is a supreme.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:35.900
Speaker 2: It's a Supreme Being.

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:36.380
Speaker 2: It's a.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:37.660
Speaker 2: It's a common narrative.

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:39.330
Speaker 2: Narrative across board.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:39.810
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:44.210
Speaker 2: And I think it begs the question of the fact that it's God.

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:48.690
Speaker 2: So whatever evolves over time, it's very important that we investigate it.

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:49.410
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:13:00.970
Speaker 2: To see where these beliefs stem from and to investigate that if there has always been an ultimate belief of a supreme God, we can't just put every other God in that same bucket.

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:01.410
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:17.210
Speaker 1: And I think it's very profound, what you just said, because now what they believe about the Supreme Being is the issue, because Africans have this view of the Supreme Being as someone who is transcendence and handed over everything to lesser deities.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:21.610
Speaker 1: So in some mythologies, he did not even do the creating work himself.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.930
Speaker 1: He handed it over to someone.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:28.290
Speaker 1: Maybe there was a particular deity that made the form and another con came.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:30.010
Speaker 1: Made the ant, and another came.

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:33.940
Speaker 1: Made the eyes and stuff like that, just to show how.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:38.370
Speaker 1: How detached they are from the Supreme Being.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:40.250
Speaker 1: And I think that says a lot.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:41.210
Speaker 1: It.

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:44.970
Speaker 1: From their mythology, it shows that they are more familiar with the lesser deities.

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:46.810
Speaker 2: Than the Supreme Being himself, because they.

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:48.170
Speaker 1: Actually know nothing about him.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:51.690
Speaker 2: This is really important.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:14:04.340
Speaker 2: And in the discourse of faith and spirituality as we are speaking to it, just understanding this alone is fundamental to how we perceive who these lesser gods are.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:04.660
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:08.060
Speaker 2: Or perceive the practices of this African traditional religion.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:11.220
Speaker 2: And is fundamental to how we can bring them into the faith.

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:12.020
Speaker 1: Exactly.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:23.220
Speaker 1: And just to even add to that, if you study in African traditional religion, some of these lesser deities are actually historical figures.

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:24.660
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:33.770
Speaker 1: For instance, Shango is rumored, or at least according to some scholars, is said to be the third Alafi of Moyo.

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:41.290
Speaker 1: So this is someone we can trace to a particular time in history, meaning before the time of Shongo, who were you worshipping?

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:44.890
Speaker 2: And is it possible that these people deified themselves?

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:47.130
Speaker 1: Exactly.

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:49.450
Speaker 1: So these are the questions.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:53.050
Speaker 1: These are the questions that really demand answer.

00:14:53.050 --> 00:15:01.800
Speaker 1: So from what you would, you will see, you understand that African traditional religion was not set in stone at a particular time in history.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.440
Speaker 1: This is something that has constantly.
